WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.380
Lindsay Levin: So we're living in an era
with multiple overlapping disruptions

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.980
that business is facing,
and I want to dive straight in

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.620
and talk about one of the biggest
of those, which is AI.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.140
How are you approaching AI?

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.300
Paul Hudson: You know, AI at scale,
it's a whole big subject, of course,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.660
but for us, at Sanofi,

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:25.380
we aim to be the world's leading
pharmaceutical company using AI at scale.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.660
Why and how are we going to do that?

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.020
It's pretty straightforward.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.340
We have 23,000 people using AI
as often as every month,

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:38.300
9,000 people in the company
using AI as often as every day.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.980
We're boldly taking on the opportunity
to completely disrupt the business.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.180
We don't have a choice.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.020
It's the fourth industrial revolution.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.180
It's here whether we like it or not.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.940
And it's amazing how resistant people
can be across organizations

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.740
and across industries.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.420
But we're all in and have been
quite public about that.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.340
Our aim is to provide
daily decision intelligence,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.500
to give people a sort of nudge
in the right direction,

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.300
to give them deeper insights,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.740
to allow them to be more
effective at what they do.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.940
And it's real.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.180
And it's such a privilege
to be involved in it.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.860
LL: I mean, you're taking
a very aggressive, active stance.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.180
What surprised you?

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.500
PH: Well, a lot of things
surprise you about AI.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.540
I mean, for some people
it's Skynet and Terminator.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.300
For some people,
they confuse AI with cyber.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.580
I'm not saying everything is perfect,

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:34.100
but I'm surprised at the number
of CEOs or executives who --

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.980
Their first response
to an AI conversation is

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.260
"Governance, controls, rules, principles."

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.500
Of course, everything has its place,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.820
but I think we have to be
honest with ourselves.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:48.060
If it is the fourth industrial revolution,
which we believe it is,

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.740
then hesitating,

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.180
this fear that can take over,

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.940
can deprive you of so much opportunity.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.460
And you have to go for it.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:01.620
I find that when you talk to lots of CEOs,
they really are very hesitant.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.660
Some would say even frightened.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.460
I look internally,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.060
people are frightened that you get
this radical data transparency

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.820
You can see their data real-time.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.940
LL: And you're experiencing that.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.900
PH: I've experienced that and still do.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.980
You know, people are often shocked

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.460
that you may get the insight
at the same time

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.540
as somebody lower down the organization.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.980
And then there's the lost opportunity
to polish a slide deck

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.620
and re-present it in the way
that I'm supposed to be informed.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.820
It's not a deliberate,
sort of, misleading approach.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.740
It's what people know.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.580
They get the insight,
they craft the story,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.780
they push it upwards.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.100
And that's life in many corporations.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.700
For us, we get the data,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.380
I get the same data every level
of the organization does.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.740
I get the insight exactly the same time.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:55.940
And then people say, "Paul, don't look,
the data is not 100 percent correct."

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.260
Well, make it correct
because the data is live.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.020
So if you really jump in
and make it correct,

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.340
it'll better reflect
what you're doing, right?

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.900
But if we wait for perfection
it's simply not going to happen.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:14.140
LL: So we're seeing fear and some
of that, I guess, is not unreasonable.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.940
You know, we read reports
about the impact on job losses,

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:19.140
for example,

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:20.380
to come from AI.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.780
I wonder what mindset you believe
people need to adopt

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.300
in the workforce of all generations,

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.820
as they approach or we all
approach this new future?

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.900
PH: You know, the adoption
of AI in particular is not about jobs.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:37.860
And I know people will think that

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.740
and inevitably, more meaningful
work is created.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.460
And of course, some roles change
or some skills don't match.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:48.020
And therefore, you know, with the help
of many of the people in the room,

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.220
you get to reshape organizations.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.740
But in the end,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.900
it's really about using
artificial intelligence

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:59.460
to create this real momentum
of decision making

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.620
and to be able to take such an advantage
over the competition.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.100
And we believe, I believe,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.980
that if you create more meaningful work

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.900
and people focus on insights and action

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:13.100
and less on Excel and PowerPoint and Word,

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.940
then there is a chance
that they enjoy their work more.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:19.060
Now it may lead to productivity gains,
it may lead to efficiency.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.460
It may lead to all those things,
nobody's sort of denying that.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.780
But what I've discovered
so far is when it does,

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.740
people see it quite quickly

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.180
and they put their hand up
to do something else,

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.220
or to focus more on insights

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.660
than ... data crunching

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.180
and aggregation.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.420
You know, I'm old enough to remember
when the internet was launched,

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.940
you know, commercially.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.020
And it's sort of similar arguments,
even when the cell phone was launched.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.460
"Be careful."

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.180
But the truth is,
they made all our lives better.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.260
The use cases are coming.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.820
But I think we're starting
to understand now

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.380
how much this is going to change
everybody's lives.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.740
LL: So who is leading this
in the organization?

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.460
Is it a new generation?

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.660
Is it specially appointed people?

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.900
Where's the leadership coming from?

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.380
PH: It's an excellent question

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.820
because maybe I'm the last
of the great dinosaur CEOs

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:18.340
who got to the top
by doing sort of every job.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.860
I ran Japan, I ran North America,

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.020
you know, I was in global marketing.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.940
You know, I've done all the tasks,
to get to the top,

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:30.260
and then I've sort of seen everything.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.940
And so I can be involved
in every discussion.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:38.020
And now the younger talent are saying,

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:39.780
"You didn't see AI, old man."

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:44.100
So, you know, "I have a better
insight than you do."

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:48.260
And "Oh, and by the way, I'm not just
going to push it up to my boss,

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:49.820
I'd like to tell you myself."

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.060
LL: Right.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.660
PH: So the younger talent,
justifiably is saying,

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.460
"Hey," you know, "I don't need to have
my work shared upwards

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.220
by a bunch of guys

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:02.820
who are all sitting there
going, 'What do you think?'

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.420
and none of them actually know."

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.380
And so we invert the pyramid.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:09.100
We have to have different
people with two, three,

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:11.500
four years experience in the room.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.220
Because what do we know?

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:15.940
And that's sort of exciting,
I think, really exciting.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.140
LL: So is AI a unique disruptor?

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:22.060
If we think about some of the other
big challenges, you know,

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:25.740
we've got to shift the entire global
economy to be sustainable, for example.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.700
Is that comparable in terms of complexity?

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.060
Maybe more so?

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.660
How do you tackle that kind of an issue?

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:37.420
PH: I think these are the big
transformational moments for society.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.700
And, you know, sustainability is,

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:43.300
you know, for many,
it was carbon neutrality,

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:44.500
then it was net zero,

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.420
it was go to COP 28,

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.900
it was put a poster by the elevator

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:52.660
with the meaningful work you're doing
to show your commitment.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.500
But it's really different now.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.860
I think there's a collective realization,
certainly in healthcare,

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.180
that we didn't do well enough.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.780
And we're a very
purpose-driven organization --

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.180
an industry, in fact.
We do health,

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:11.140
we transform the practice of medicine,
we invent miracles often.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.620
And so it's very easy to say,
look, we're very purpose-driven.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:20.420
But it doesn't abdicate the responsibility

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:24.660
of removing plastic from packaging
of vaccines and medicines.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.620
It's ridiculous to even think
you wouldn't have to.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.780
Often it's harder with the regulator,
by the way, to get that done

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.500
than with your own people.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.420
LL: So you've got those kinds
of projects going on, have you?

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:36.780
Can you give us an example?

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:37.980
PH: We have to do it.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:41.660
We have to do it because, you know,
we have this sort of approach

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.580
of what can we do

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:46.780
that if we don't do, it won't happen?

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.460
And that's sort of our philosophy.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.620
You know, in healthcare,
it's a good example,

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:55.100
you know, delivery of healthcare
creates more carbon

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.100
than the airline industry.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.340
And that's half of that,
let's say five percent.

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:03.140
Half of that is making drugs,
shipping them, doing different things.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.420
The other half is people driving
to hospitals for an appointment

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:10.620
in an overheated, overcooled
healthcare practice, too often,

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:13.860
without the use of a virtual
hybrid delivery of healthcare.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:17.060
And it's the same as the airline industry.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.940
And, you know, we feel,
because we're in healthcare,

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.860
we have this unique opportunity.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:24.380
If somebody is pre-diabetic, for example,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:27.220
and you coach them
and they change their lifestyle

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.100
and don't become a diabetic,

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:33.740
that's a difference of them
creating 16 tons of carbon

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.860
as a normal adult, healthy,

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:40.180
and 48 tons of carbon in their adult life
if they become diabetic.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:42.740
That's a 3X.

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:45.300
That's really meaningful.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:46.940
And if we don't step in and help,

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.860
we just simply never get there
and we're doing a lot of work,

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.860
I'm doing a lot of work with King Charles

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.940
and the Sustainable Markets Initiative

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.420
to get people to decarbonize
the delivery of healthcare,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.540
because it's such a massive opportunity.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.740
LL: And presumably you've got
to collaborate in very different ways

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:04.420
than in the past to do, you know,

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:07.940
you're talking about supply chains
to deal with something like plastic.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.820
Are you seeing different kinds
of skills from people

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:13.700
in order to make those collaborations?

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:17.860
PH: Well, I think these functions
and the sustainability groups, as I said,

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.820
have come from a poster by the elevator

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:23.580
to being very actively involved in a lot
of work to do these things properly.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:28.100
And, you know, you, it's not
about a competitive advantage,

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:32.140
in Sanofi being better
at wastewater management

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:36.700
or renewable energy
than Pfizer or AstraZeneca.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:38.620
That's not a competition.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:42.460
The competition is us versus,
you know, destroying the planet.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.740
So we work a lot together
to do the right thing.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:50.860
I work with Novo Nordisk, AstraZeneca, GSK

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:54.460
to try and work out ways
to be kinder to the environment

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:57.540
in the delivery of health,
and it's the right thing to do, right?

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:01.220
It's a shared responsibility,
a collective responsibility.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.220
LL: And we're talking here
about big social challenges

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:06.140
beyond any one business

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.740
or industry or even country,

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:12.020
with an expectation that business
needs to step forward.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.140
I think partly driven by the fact
that policy doesn't always work,

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.940
and we're disappointed with leadership
and with government.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.060
So a lot of finger pointing
as to who's responsible

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.620
and who can act on these big shifts.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:27.580
I wonder how ambitious and bold

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:32.220
do you feel CEOs should be
about stepping forward

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:36.660
and helping society through some
of these mega transitions

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:38.260
that we are faced with?

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:43.180
PH: You know, it's clear
that companies are being pulled more

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.220
into the conversation
about individual's values.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:51.300
And I think people
who work in our company,

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:52.660
and all companies,

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:55.620
start to try and identify
themselves, perhaps rightly,

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:57.660
with the values of the company.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:00.580
And they're starting to have
much higher expectations

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:03.140
about the company they work for.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:07.180
And it can be on all
the major social issues.

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:10.900
You know, there's been so many difficult
moments and tragic moments.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:14.100
I'm often written to by people
from all over the world.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.980
"You haven't declared which side
you're on on this important subject.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:19.180
Why not?"

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:23.300
And people want to know
that you are fully vested

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:25.460
and the company is behind them.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:28.820
To be clear, it's almost impossible
to get everything right.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.700
The world is almost,
you know, perfectly divided.

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.700
You can pick an issue and half
your employees will tell you

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.860
"We don't agree," and the other
half will say, "Well done."

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:40.980
And we're not used to that as CEOs,

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:44.820
we're used to trying to find
the right sort of moment

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.180
to get the majority to say,
"I'm proud of my company."

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.340
So you have to retreat
a little bit and say,

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:55.900
I'll spend my energy on making sure
whatever the issue, that the people,

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:58.260
90,000 people in our case,

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:01.900
get the very best chance to live
their best version of themselves.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.020
Could be inclusion, it could be race,

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:06.980
it could be many different things.

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:09.300
It could be LGBTQ-plus.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.260
But whatever those issues
that are being debated strongly

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:15.740
or less strongly in different
parts of the world,

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:18.180
it matters to us that our people feel

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.220
they can be the best
version of themselves.

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:22.660
So when people ask,
"What do you think about this?"

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:24.180
"What do you think about that?"

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.740
I can have a personal opinion,
but our organization,

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:29.100
if you're looking to match
your values with ours,

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.300
is really about, we're in healthcare,

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:33.660
we want you to have
the best life possible.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:39.780
How can the company facilitate that
in this sort of, maelstrom of subjects?

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:42.180
And we focus then --
I'm not saying we're perfect,

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:44.620
I don't think any company really
is perfect on this,

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:50.940
but the expectation of our own employees
to be able to set a standard on an issue

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:53.060
and to see it through is real.

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:55.700
And leading in these times
is more complicated, I think,

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:57.460
than perhaps it has been previously.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:59.340
LL: So you're describing a world

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:02.980
where the work of the CEO
is changing very fast.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:08.740
Could you just sum up for us
the role of a leader in this new era?

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:13.020
PH: Well, I took this role
at the end of 2019 and thought,

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:15.020
I will roll through my 100-day plan

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:19.940
and I will amble through getting around
town halls across the world

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:22.940
and shake a lot of hands
and do a listening tour.

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:26.820
And a pandemic dropped on us
within a few weeks after that

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:31.300
and worked out of the kitchen
not far from here.

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:34.620
And the war, Russia-Ukraine,

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.740
current war, Israel and Hamas,

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.260
and mentioned the pandemic.

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:45.820
China-US, the social issues.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:49.100
And I think what we realize is leading

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:53.380
is I think somebody described it
as the perma crisis, you know,

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:55.940
a sort of, you know, a cadence of issues

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.300
that just is relentless.

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:03.380
And you really have to have some
resiliency leading now, I think,

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:07.860
and you have to recognize that there are
a series of sprints in the role.

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:11.020
There's the fundamentals of the business
that must be continued.

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:12.780
Then there's a metronome for us.

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:15.300
We feel a responsibility
to bring medicines

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:19.220
and transformational medicines
forward is non-negotiable.

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:20.620
At the same time,

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:24.420
parts of your organization
is in a very difficult situation

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:25.780
somewhere in the world.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:29.620
And we have to make sure
we have the right energy, experts,

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:33.580
support, crisis teams, often,
more recently

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:36.180
to protect our people

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:37.780
and to continue the work we do.

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:43.180
We ship drugs all over the world,
irrespective of the stance of politics

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:44.380
or anything else.

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:46.700
People are people
and if they're sick we'll help.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:48.660
But it really has got very complicated.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.900
So resiliency, agility
and being open-minded,

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:55.060
recognizing you're not
the expert many times,

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:57.380
trusting the advice you get
from your own people,

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:59.020
particularly those on the ground,

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:01.100
protecting your people
where it's necessary,

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:03.820
moving your people where it's necessary.

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:06.420
That's the sort of new
expectation, really.

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:10.500
LL: I mean, I think we're all experiencing
this sense of perma crisis.

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:12.060
Just to finish,

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:14.180
it would be great to just get a sense

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:16.580
of what are you really
excited about right now?

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:20.180
PH: Well, I'm incredibly excited,

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:23.700
I touched on it at the beginning
about the use of artificial intelligence,

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.460
particularly large language models.

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:28.260
Because I think it changes everything.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.620
It's got me questioning
whether I can go back

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:33.260
and look at medicines
that didn't quite make it,

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.620
and wonder if we just didn't know enough

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:38.540
with the data that we had
to look deep enough.

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:42.100
It can be, you know,
recently we just did our --

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:44.140
this is a small example,
but it's just fun,

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.900
our engagement survey,

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:49.100
we had a 409,000 comments,

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:50.940
9 million words.

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:53.940
And normally, somebody
would make a nice slide deck

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:56.980
to tell me, "The organization poll
is very engaged.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:15:58.900
Never been more engaged."

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:00.540
"How much more engaged?" "0.1."

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:02.660
"OK, good, thank you."

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:04.420
Definitely improvement.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:07.900
And so I asked them
to run the 409,000 comments

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:09.460
through a large language model.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:12.020
Forty minutes later, it told me
the three things

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:13.700
that people love about the company

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.820
and three things
that people hate about it.

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:20.460
I didn't need a lot of external support,
didn't need teams of people.

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:22.660
And it was clear, it was no hallucination,

00:16:22.700 --> 00:16:24.820
because it was there right in front of me.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:26.020
And it made sense.

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:29.300
And I shared them with people
and they're like, yeah, that's us.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:32.220
And I think that's the difference
between meaningful work

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.780
using -- let's talk about what it tells us

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:37.500
about whether our people
really like it here

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:39.260
and bring their best or not.

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.820
I think AI for me, a relatively new CEO,

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:47.340
I have a chance to disrupt structural
biology, electron microscopy,

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:49.060
I have a chance to invent medicines

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:51.620
and druggable targets
that were never touched before.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:54.580
I have a chance to take away the sort of,

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:56.860
I should put it, the heavy lifting,

00:16:56.900 --> 00:17:00.420
unglamorous work
that people don't like doing.

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:02.780
I've a chance to reinvent everything,

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:05.020
to do it more efficiently,
reinvest in R and D.

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:07.980
And I have a chance to get ahead
of the competition

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:10.700
while they're all worrying,

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:12.580
we have governance,

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:14.740
but every step forward by us

00:17:14.740 --> 00:17:18.020
is a step of leaving behind
those that are overly sensitive,

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:19.620
and we're happy to share.

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:21.700
But we can do incredible
things for patients

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:23.300
and for the people in the company

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:26.020
by being more bold
about stepping into the new world.

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:27.700
LL: Your passion is infectious.

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:28.900
Thank you.

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:30.540
Thank you very much, Paul.

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:31.780
PH: Thank you.

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:33.420
(Applause)

